Here's a lesson program that makes the Hessian's role in the second derivative test visible by restricting the surface to a rotatable slice through the critical point.

```ts
/**
 * Why the second derivative test classifies critical points.
 * At a critical point the gradient dies, so the local shape is the quadratic
 * form (1/2) xᵀHx. Rotate a slicing direction through the point: the sign of
 * its curvature is uᵀHu. Positive in every direction (both eigenvalues > 0) is
 * a minimum; mixed signs is a saddle. det H = λ₁λ₂ encodes which case you're in.
 */

// ── shared setup: a family of surfaces whose curvature signs the student controls ──
// f(x,y) = a x² + b y², critical point at the origin. a,b are the eigenvalues.
const a = M.parameter("a", 1, { range: [-2, 2] });   // curvature along the x-axis
const b = M.parameter("b", 1, { range: [-2, 2] });   // curvature along the y-axis
const f = Field.scalarField((x, y) => a.value * x * x + b.value * y * y, { label: "f" });

// direction of a line through the origin: (cos θ, sin θ)
const theta = M.parameter("theta", 0, { range: [0, Math.PI] });
// f restricted to that line, as a function of arclength t — theta read INSIDE, so it is live
const slice = Calc.fn(
  (t) => f.at(t * Math.cos(theta.value), t * Math.sin(theta.value)),
  { label: "f along the slice" }
);

// the Hessian at the critical point and its spectral data
const H = Field.hessian(f, [0, 0]);          // [[2a,0],[0,2b]]
const eig = LinAlg.eigen(H);
const detH = LinAlg.det(H);

lesson.beat("Flat where the gradient dies");

const grad = Field.gradient(f);
const fieldView = View.field(grad, { domain: [-1.5, 1.5], range: [-1.5, 1.5], label: "\\nabla f" });
lesson.show(fieldView);
const gradMag = grad.magnitudeAt(0, 0);
const flat = M.claim({
  lhs: gradMag,
  rhs: 0,
  relation: "≈",
  tolerance: 1e-6,
  label: "|\\nabla f| = 0 at the critical point",
});
lesson.show(View.claim(flat));
lesson.say`Look at the gradient ${grad} of ${f}. Right at the critical point the origin, it vanishes — ${gradMag} is zero. The tangent plane is perfectly horizontal there, so the FIRST derivatives can't tell you whether you're sitting in a bowl or on a saddle. We need to look deeper.`;

lesson.beat("Zoom in on the bowl");

const quad = F.tex`${F.span("f", "f")}(x,y) \approx f(0,0) + \tfrac12 ${F.span("form", "x^{T} H x")}`;
lesson.show(View.formula(quad));
lesson.say`Zoom in close. Because the gradient died, the leading behavior of ${f} near the point is the quadratic form ${quad.span("form")} from its Taylor expansion — the constant and linear terms carry no shape, so ALL the min-vs-saddle information sits in that matrix ${H}.`;

lesson.beat("Curvature lives in the Hessian");

const hessianView = View.matrix(H);
lesson.show(hessianView);
lesson.say`${H} is the Hessian: the matrix of second partial derivatives. Each entry measures how the surface bends. For our ${f} it is diagonal, with the pure curvatures ${H.cell(0,0)} and ${H.cell(1,1)} down its diagonal — the surface bends independently along the two axes.`;

lesson.beat("Eigenvectors are the pure directions");

// a slice plot sharing the axes with a plane so the rotating direction is visible
const plane = View.coordinatePlane({ grid: true, domain: [-1.5, 1.5] });
const dir = LinAlg.basisVector(2, 0);   // shown, then the reader rotates theta
const dirView = View.vectors([dir], { labels: ["u"] });
const slicePlot = View.plot(slice, { domain: [-1.2, 1.2], label: "curvature along u" });
lesson.show(Layout.columns([Layout.overlay([plane, dirView]), slicePlot]));
lesson.show(View.readout(eig.values[0], { label: "\\lambda_1" }));
lesson.show(View.readout(eig.values[1], { label: "\\lambda_2" }));
lesson.say`The eigenvectors of ${H} are the special directions where the surface bends purely, with no twist. Their eigenvalues ${eig.values[0]} and ${eig.values[1]} are the bending RATES. The right panel plots ${f} along a chosen direction u — a slice through the surface — so we can watch its curvature directly.`;

lesson.beat("Two ups make a bowl");

// force positive definite: both curvatures up
a.setValue(1);
b.setValue(1);
const upEverywhere = M.claim({
  predicate: () => {
    const u = [Math.cos(theta.value), Math.sin(theta.value)];
    // uᵀHu — the curvature of the slice in direction u
    return H.cell(0,0).value*u[0]*u[0] + 2*H.cell(0,1).value*u[0]*u[1] + H.cell(1,1).value*u[1]*u[1] > 0;
  },
  label: "the slice curves UPWARD in every direction",
});
lesson.show(View.claim(upEverywhere));
lesson.say`Set both curvatures positive: ${eig.values[0]} and ${eig.values[1]} are both above zero, so ${H} is positive definite. The slice curvature u^T H u is positive whichever way u points — the surface is a bowl, and the point is a local MINIMUM. This badge never breaks no matter how you rotate the slice.`;

lesson.beat("One up, one down: saddle");

// flip one eigenvalue negative: mixed signs
b.setValue(-1.5);
lesson.say`Now flip ${b} negative, so the eigenvalues ${eig.values[0]} and ${eig.values[1]} have OPPOSITE signs. Along one eigenvector the slice curves up; along the other it curves DOWN — up one way, down the other. That is a saddle, and ${upEverywhere} now fails for some directions: rotate the slice and watch its curvature change sign.`;

lesson.beat("Determinant reads the signs");

const detClaimMin = M.claim({
  predicate: () => detH.value > 0 && H.cell(0,0).value > 0,
  label: "det H > 0 and f_{xx} > 0  \\Rightarrow  minimum",
});
const detReadsSaddle = M.claim({
  predicate: () => detH.value < 0,
  label: "det H < 0  \\Rightarrow  saddle",
});
lesson.show(View.readout(detH, { label: "\\det H = \\lambda_1 \\lambda_2" }));
lesson.show(View.claim(detClaimMin));
lesson.show(View.claim(detReadsSaddle));
lesson.expose(a, { as: "sliders" });
lesson.expose(b, { as: "sliders" });
lesson.expose(theta, { as: "sliders" });
lesson.say`The determinant ${detH} is the PRODUCT of the eigenvalues, so its sign alone reads the situation: ${detReadsSaddle} — a negative product means one eigenvalue is negative, forcing mixed signs. And ${detClaimMin} — a positive product with an upward f_{xx} forces BOTH positive. Drag ${a} and ${b} to move the eigenvalues across zero, and rotate ${theta} to slice the surface any way you like: the badges flip exactly at the sign changes. That is the whole second derivative test.`;
```
